Password Reset

A password reset is performed when a user forgets their password or cannot access their account.

The IT administrator resets the password to a temporary password and asks the user to create a new password after logging in.

Password Reset Process
Verify the user's identity according to company policy.
Open Active Directory Users and Computers (ADUC).
Search for the user account.
Select Reset Password.
Assign a temporary password.
Enable User must change password at next logon if required by company policy.
Provide the temporary password securely.
Verify the user can successfully log in.
Account Lockout

A user account becomes locked after multiple failed login attempts.

Possible reasons:

Incorrect password entered several times.
Keyboard issues such as Caps Lock.
Cached old password on another device.
Resolution
Verify the user's identity.
Locate the user account in Active Directory.
Unlock the account.
Reset the password if necessary.
Verify successful login.
Disable User Account

A disabled account cannot log in but still exists in Active Directory.

Common reasons:

Employee resignation
Long-term leave
Security investigation
Temporary suspension

Disabling the account preserves company data while preventing access.

Difference Between Disable and Delete
Disable
User account remains in Active Directory.
Company data is preserved.
Access is blocked.
Delete
User account is permanently removed.
Usually performed according to company retention policy.
Not commonly done immediately after an employee leaves.


